"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkakoflow_docs||=[]).push([[9169],{41564(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"akoflow-hub-is-alive","metadata":{"permalink":"/akoflow/blog/akoflow-hub-is-alive","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-16-akoflow-hub-is-alive.mdx","title":"Ak\xf4Flow Hub is alive \u2014 publish, discover and reuse workflows","description":"A step-by-step tour of Ak\xf4Flow Hub: explore public experiments, export workflows, publish execution results and import a version into Desktop.","date":"2026-11-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Desktop","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/desktop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Experiments","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/experiments"}],"readingTime":9.06,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Wesley Ferreira","title":"@ovvesley","url":"https://github.com/ovvesley","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12123871?v=4&size=96","key":"ovvesley","page":null},{"name":"Boosted with LLMs","key":"llms","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"akoflow-hub-is-alive","authors":["ovvesley","llms"],"title":"Ak\xf4Flow Hub is alive \u2014 publish, discover and reuse workflows","description":"A step-by-step tour of Ak\xf4Flow Hub: explore public experiments, export workflows, publish execution results and import a version into Desktop.","image":"/img/blog/hub-is-alive/01-explore.png","tags":["Desktop","Experiments"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.14 \u2014 global search, audit and infrastructure navigation","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/akoflow-1-0-14"}},"content":"**Ak\xf4Flow Hub is alive.** Open [hub.akoflow.com](https://hub.akoflow.com) to discover workflows, inspect their graphs and recorded experiments, and bring a published definition into your own Ak\xf4Flow Desktop.\\n\\nYou can now share the workflow behind a result, keep its versions together and let someone else start from the same definition. Here is the complete journey: discover \u2192 export \u2192 publish \u2192 import \u2192 prepare a local run.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![Ak\xf4Flow Hub homepage with public workflows and research categories](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/01-explore.png)\\n\\n## What is available today?\\n\\nThe public Hub brings together workflow definitions, README instructions, immutable workflow versions, executable-image references and recorded execution results. Depending on what the author included, an experiment can also show activity observations, timing, resource metrics, process output and file references.\\n\\nStart with the official [Hyperparameter search](https://hub.akoflow.com/o/akoflow/machine-learning-hyperparameter-search) example used in this tutorial. Its six activities prepare a dataset, run three training trials, compare their measured errors and register the best model.\\n\\nThe catalog also includes astronomical image mosaicking, OpenOil ensembles, model conversion, RAG pipelines, agent workflows, document processing and rendering. Browse by category or search for a workflow that matches your work. The [Showcase](/docs/showcase) connects examples to their source and supporting evidence.\\n\\n![A public workflow with its import reference, description and activity graph](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/02-publication.png)\\n\\n**Ak\xf4Flow Hub and Docker Hub have different jobs.** Ak\xf4Flow Hub shares workflows and experiment evidence. Docker Hub supplies container images that activities can use. Workflows may also reference images from other registries; this tutorial\'s example uses an immutable image from GHCR.\\n\\n## Before you begin\\n\\nUse a current Ak\xf4Flow Desktop and daemon with the Hub controls shown below. You can browse public publications without signing in. Publishing requires a Hub account, a destination project and a finished local execution. Importing creates a local definition; running it also requires compatible infrastructure and access to the workflow\'s images and inputs.\\n\\nThese screenshots come from the live public Hub and a working local Desktop. The publishing walkthrough uses a new **private** tutorial publication. Its result was uploaded successfully, and the import walkthrough creates a separate local copy from that same private publication. Public Hub screenshots show the official example; the Desktop screenshots use our tutorial reference on the local Hub service. The GIFs replay captured steps; pause on any still image for the details. No new execution or infrastructure provisioning was started while recording.\\n\\n## Export a workflow as YAML\\n\\n1. In Desktop, open **Workflows \u2192 Definitions** and select a workflow.\\n2. Select **Export YAML**, the download action beside the workflow title.\\n3. Save the `.yaml` file. This exports the portable definition, including its activities and dependencies.\\n\\n![Workflow definition and the Export YAML download action](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/05-export-yaml.png)\\n\\nA YAML export carries the definition. It does not package your execution history, local infrastructure or datasets. To share recorded results with the definition, use **Publish to Hub** below. To move just the file to another Desktop, select **Import YAML** in its workflow catalog.\\n\\n## Publish a finished execution to Ak\xf4Flow Hub\\n\\n### 1. Connect Desktop to your Hub project\\n\\nOpen **Settings \u2192 Ak\xf4Flow Hub** and select **Sign in to Ak\xf4Flow Hub**. Continue in the browser, check the displayed authorization code, sign in and authorize the destination project. Desktop detects approval automatically.\\n\\nWhen ready, the settings page shows **Connected to Ak\xf4Flow Hub** and the selected destination. You enter your Hub password in the browser, and the connection is scoped to the authorized project.\\n\\n![Desktop settings showing a connected Hub account and destination project; account email masked](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/04-connection.png)\\n\\n### 2. Open the result you want to share\\n\\nOpen a finished execution and select **Publish to Hub**. This publishes a snapshot of that execution with the workflow version it actually used, even if you edited the definition afterward.\\n\\n![A completed execution with the Publish to Hub action](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/06-completed-run.png)\\n\\n### 3. Choose or create a publication\\n\\nChoose an existing destination when you want to add evidence to the same publication. For a new workflow, select **New publication**, enter a title, review the generated slug and choose its visibility. Select **Create publication** to continue.\\n\\nPublications start **private**. A public publication makes the selected content available to other people; choose that visibility when you intend to share it openly.\\n\\n![New publication form with title, editable slug and private visibility](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/08-new-publication.png)\\n\\n### 4. Select the evidence to include\\n\\nThe workflow definition, scheduling context and execution summary are always included. Add a version message, then choose any optional sections:\\n\\n| Option           | What it adds                                               |\\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| Process logs     | Recorded output from activities.                           |\\n| File references  | Recorded file metadata and locations.                      |\\n| Activity metrics | Detailed resource measurements collected during execution. |\\n| Execution events | Recorded events and execution timeline.                    |\\n\\nAll four options start unchecked. File references describe files; their contents are not uploaded by this publishing flow. Full datasets and binary artifacts stay outside the capture.\\n\\n![Publication destination, version message and optional evidence controls](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/07-publish-selection.png)\\n\\n### 5. Review, then publish\\n\\nSelect **Review result**. Inspect the destination, visibility, workflow YAML, version changes and any selected logs or file references. Review the content even when automatic redaction reports no secrets.\\n\\n![Review of a private execution capture with workflow YAML, process logs and workflow changes](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/09-review.png)\\n\\nSelect **Publish reviewed result**. Public destinations also require you to acknowledge making the capture public. Wait for **Result published**, then select **Open publication in Ak\xf4Flow Hub** to get its link.\\n\\n![Successful publication receipt with a link to open the publication](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/09b-published.png)\\n\\nThe Hub keeps workflow versions immutable and attaches captured results to their version. A new execution can add evidence without requiring you to change the workflow definition.\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>Watch the export and publication steps</summary>\\n\\n![Step replay: export YAML, open a completed execution, create a private publication, review its evidence and view the publication receipt](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/export-and-publish.gif)\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n## Import a published workflow into Desktop\\n\\n### 1. Choose a workflow version in the Hub\\n\\nOpen a public publication and find **Import into Desktop**. Choose **v1** for a fixed version, or **Latest** when you want the version that is newest at import time. Select **Copy URI**.\\n\\nFor the official example on the public Hub service, the fixed reference is:\\n\\n```text\\nakoflow::o/akoflow/machine-learning-hyperparameter-search:v1\\n```\\n\\nThe Desktop screenshots use `akoflow::ovvesley/akoflow-hub-tutorial-hyperparameter-search:v1`, our private demonstration copy. Copy a reference you can access from your own configured Hub service rather than typing the demonstration reference.\\n\\nThe `o/akoflow` part identifies the organization. Personal publications use the author\'s username instead. The final `:v1` selects a workflow version; `:latest` resolves the latest available version. Record the resolved version when you need to repeat an experiment.\\n\\n![An immutable Hub workflow version and its version-specific import URI](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/03-version.png)\\n\\n### 2. Load the definition in Desktop\\n\\nOpen **Workflows \u2192 Definitions \u2192 Create workflow**, choose **Import from Hub**, paste the reference into **Hub workflow URI** and select **Load definition**.\\n\\n![Desktop Import from Hub form with a versioned publication reference](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/10-import-reference.png)\\n\\n### 3. Review the YAML and name your copy\\n\\nCheck the resolved reference and version. Expand **Preview workflow YAML** to inspect the activities and commands. Desktop suggests a unique local name; keep it or enter your own.\\n\\n![Loaded workflow definition with a YAML preview control and a local workflow name](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/11-import-review.png)\\n\\nSelect **Import workflow definition**. Desktop opens the imported workflow, where you can inspect its graph and generate a plan.\\n\\n![Imported six-activity workflow open in Desktop](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/12-imported-workflow.png)\\n\\nImporting creates your own local definition. Execution history, environments and datasets remain at the source. It also leaves the author\'s publication unchanged.\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>Watch the import steps</summary>\\n\\n![Step replay: find the Hub version, paste its reference, review the YAML and open the imported local workflow](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/import-workflow.gif)\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n## Make the imported workflow runnable\\n\\nAn imported graph is the starting point. Before selecting **Generate plan**, open the publication\'s README and **Images & environment** section to check its requirements.\\n\\nFor Hyperparameter search:\\n\\n1. Open the [example source directory](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow-examples/tree/main/machine-learning/hyperparameter-search) and follow its local setup instructions.\\n2. Make the declared executable image available to your runtime. Preserve its immutable digest when reproducing the published capture.\\n3. Register the companion `environment.yaml`, `scope.yaml` and `topology.yaml` in the order documented by the example. Check that the environment\'s resource, runtime and workspace paths match your machine.\\n4. In Desktop, open the imported workflow, select **Generate plan**, choose the prepared execution scope and run **HEFT**. Review a feasible candidate.\\n5. Select **Execute plan**, confirm **Real execution**, and follow the run until it completes. Check the activity outcomes and generated files before publishing your result.\\n\\nFor an introduction to local execution, follow [From a DAG to your first run](/blog/from-a-dag-to-your-first-run). Your timing and outputs should be assessed against your own inputs and environment; a published result is a record of its original run.\\n\\n### Where Docker Hub fits\\n\\nWhen building or adapting an activity, open **Choose an artifact \u2192 Import from Docker Hub**. Search for a public repository, choose a tag and a compatible Linux architecture, then review the artifact ID and version. The import registers the artifact and builds its Apptainer SIF. Wait for verification before selecting it in the activity; monitor its artifact page if the build fails.\\n\\n![Desktop artifact picker searching public images on Docker Hub](/img/blog/hub-is-alive/13-docker-hub.png)\\n\\nDocker/OCI and SIF/Apptainer execution depend on the target environment. Choose an image that contains the tools your activity command needs. Replacing this example\'s GHCR image with a generic Python image would omit its packaged application code.\\n\\n## If a step does not work\\n\\n| What you see                           | What to check                                                                                                                        |\\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\\n| Hub is not configured                  | Ask the installation operator to configure the Hub service, then reopen Hub settings.                                                |\\n| Cannot publish                         | Connect the project in Hub settings and use a finished execution in a writable instance.                                             |\\n| Reference cannot be loaded             | Copy the URI again, verify the owner and version, and check access to private content and the installation\'s configured Hub service. |\\n| Local name already exists              | Accept Desktop\'s suggested alternative or choose another name.                                                                       |\\n| Review expired or destination changed  | Return to the selection step and request a fresh review.                                                                             |\\n| Imported workflow has no feasible plan | Check the companion environment, execution scope, resources, images and workspace configuration.                                     |\\n\\nExplore [Ak\xf4Flow Hub](https://hub.akoflow.com), import a version that interests you, and publish the evidence from your own run. The next person can start from your workflow and see how you reached the result."},{"id":"akoflow-1-0-14","metadata":{"permalink":"/akoflow/blog/akoflow-1-0-14","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-akoflow-1-0-14.mdx","title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.14 \u2014 global search, audit and infrastructure navigation","description":"Search control-plane records, explore API and runtime audit, discover keyboard shortcuts and inspect infrastructure by ownership.","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Desktop","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/desktop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Experiments","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/experiments"}],"readingTime":6.04,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Wesley Ferreira","title":"@ovvesley","url":"https://github.com/ovvesley","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12123871?v=4&size=96","key":"ovvesley","page":null},{"name":"Boosted with LLMs","key":"llms","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"akoflow-1-0-14","authors":["ovvesley","llms"],"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.14 \u2014 global search, audit and infrastructure navigation","description":"Search control-plane records, explore API and runtime audit, discover keyboard shortcuts and inspect infrastructure by ownership.","image":"/img/releases/v1.0.14/01-audit-explorer.png","tags":["Desktop","Experiments"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ak\xf4Flow Hub is alive \u2014 publish, discover and reuse workflows","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/akoflow-hub-is-alive"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.13 \u2014 workflow monitoring, files, logs and temporal events","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/akoflow-1-0-13"}},"content":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.14 adds fast global search, a keyboard shortcut reference, comprehensive audit exploration and a resource-oriented infrastructure map. These changes build on 1.0.13.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n**Database compatibility:** this release consolidates the current schema into one canonical bootstrap. Existing databases with older schema checksums are not migrated in place. Preserve a verified backup and the previous installation before switching; use a new empty database for the new writable installation. The application does not silently reset incompatible databases. Request history is stored in a separate `request-audit.db` sidecar and must be backed up separately when needed.\\n\\nThe screenshots show the actual release candidate. Audit and settings use the local installation; search and infrastructure use existing experiments opened by a separate daemon in read-only archive mode. Capturing these images did not start workflow execution, provisioning or connectivity checks. Capture provenance is stored alongside the images.\\n\\n## Explore recorded activity in Audit\\n\\nUse the event timeline, server-side counts, outcome and transport facets, configurable time ranges, grouped results and cursor pagination to investigate API access, MCP protocol activity and runtime lifecycle events. Access records include authentication outcome, request and correlation identifiers, method, status and duration. Shared tokens identify service clients, not invented human identities.\\n\\n![Audit explorer](/img/releases/v1.0.14/01-audit-explorer.png)\\n\\n## Search and combine audit filters\\n\\nSearch available filter choices, inspect recorded counts and combine filters to narrow an investigation. Additional references include request/correlation identifiers, executions, activities and operations. Loading, empty, retry and outdated-daemon states are explicit.\\n\\n![Searchable audit filter](/img/releases/v1.0.14/02-audit-filter.png)\\n\\n## Inspect request and identity evidence\\n\\nOpen an event in the shared full-height detail drawer without leaving the current results. Request, identity, target and correlation evidence stays together, with links to related records. Escape closes the drawer and restores focus. Bodies, authorization headers and secret values are not collected as audit payloads.\\n\\n![Audit event details](/img/releases/v1.0.14/03-audit-event-details.png)\\n\\n## Follow completed HTTP requests\\n\\nThe API requests view lists completed calls with method, path, status, duration, direct peer IP and caller classification. Fetch 50 records per page, navigate with cursors and refresh to return to the newest entries. Long-lived requests appear after completion. Query strings, bodies, cookies and authorization headers are omitted; caller-supplied client labels are not verified identity.\\n\\n![API request history](/img/releases/v1.0.14/04-api-request-history.png)\\n\\n## Configure request retention\\n\\nSettings exposes request-log retention from 1 to 3650 days, with a default of 30 days. Bounded periodic cleanup removes expired request records; operational audit events are preserved separately. Reducing retention can permanently remove older request records.\\n\\n![Request retention settings](/img/releases/v1.0.14/05-request-retention.png)\\n\\n## Discover shortcuts and their controls\\n\\nOpen Settings \u2192 Keyboard shortcuts, or use the shortcut icon in the top bar. The reference shows macOS and Windows/Linux combinations, where each shortcut applies and its matching button or control. Try global search directly from the reference. Contextual keys cover search results, audit menus, activity dependencies and detail panels.\\n\\n![Keyboard shortcuts](/img/releases/v1.0.14/06-keyboard-shortcuts.png)\\n\\n## Find records with global search\\n\\nPress Command/Ctrl+K or use the top-bar search field to find workflows, executions, environments and artifacts by ID or partial name. Resources, plans, execution scopes and materializations are also searchable. Results show type, version, status and owner context; owner and result links are independently clickable.\\n\\nThe daemon reads bounded metadata projections instead of loading complete catalogs, logs or workflow definitions. Results use stable cursor pagination; the interface debounces typing, cancels obsolete requests and rejects stale responses. Keyboard navigation and mobile access are supported. A local Apple M4 Pro benchmark with 100,000 execution records measured approximately 73\u2013120 ms per search; this is a local benchmark, not a universal latency guarantee.\\n\\n![Global search results](/img/releases/v1.0.14/07-global-search.png)\\n\\n## Navigate infrastructure by ownership\\n\\nThe environment map uses a vertical ownership tree with separate local, cloud and simulation branches when configured. Resources and network links have explicit entry points. Simulation adapters without bound resources do not create empty branches, and Fit view helps navigate the graph. Availability observations remain separate from configuration.\\n\\n![Environment ownership map](/img/releases/v1.0.14/08-environment-map.png)\\n\\n## Inspect resources in the shared drawer\\n\\nInspect resources and their recorded metadata in the shared full-height drawer, preserving opening/closing animations, focus handling and Escape dismissal. Search large resource lists and expand large HPC branches incrementally. Resource actions open their owning pages; on writable connected instances, available console actions hand sessions to the standard console bar instead of creating competing terminal views.\\n\\n![Resource inspector](/img/releases/v1.0.14/09-resource-inspector.png)\\n\\n## Read configured network links\\n\\nInspect configured bandwidth, latency and link metadata from the environment map. Optional animated flow respects motion preferences. Diagram arrows illustrate configured relationships, not measured live traffic. The screenshot shows archived simulation configuration.\\n\\n![Network inspector](/img/releases/v1.0.14/10-network-inspector.png)\\n\\n## Find resources without expanding every branch\\n\\nThe resource entry point opens a searchable side list. Filter recorded resources and inspect an item without losing the environment map. Available console actions on writable connected resources use the standard console bar.\\n\\n![Searchable resource list](/img/releases/v1.0.14/12-resource-list.png)\\n\\n## Keep large HPC environments readable\\n\\nLarge HPC branches initially show a small set of nodes and an explicit remaining count. Expand incrementally while keeping the full recorded inventory searchable. This archived environment contains 132 resources.\\n\\n![Large HPC overview](/img/releases/v1.0.14/13-hpc-overview.png)\\n\\n## Keep the same tools in dark mode\\n\\nAudit timelines, facets, filter menus and shared drawers use the current appearance settings in both light and dark themes.\\n\\n![Audit in dark mode](/img/releases/v1.0.14/11-audit-dark.png)\\n\\n## Other improvements\\n\\n- Workflow command fields show shell highlighting and syntax-validation state. Daemon syntax validation checks scripts without executing them.\\n- Environment editing preserves persisted identities, saved configuration and unbound resources.\\n- Audit drawers retain the shared wrapper through closing animations, and older daemon responses display actionable update guidance.\\n- The database\'s complete initial schema now includes scheduler-plugin tables; startup no longer applies the former incremental schema migrations.\\n- Global search remains available for read-only imported archives and follows the selected instance\'s data.\\n\\n## Install and update\\n\\nGet the [1.0.14 release packages](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow/releases/tag/v1.0.14) or follow the [installation guide](https://akoflow.com/docs/downloads). Desktop packages support macOS universal, Windows x64 and Linux x64. Matching daemon and BuildKit archives support Linux amd64 and arm64. Docker Desktop is required on macOS/Windows; Linux requires Docker Engine and Compose v2.\\n\\nRead the database compatibility note above before updating an existing installation. Runtime archives are loaded locally by Desktop; no container-registry deployment is required.\\n\\n## Validation\\n\\n- Daemon: complete Go suite, architecture checks and `go vet` passed.\\n- Desktop: 118 unit tests, 20 packaging/MCP/appearance contract tests, dependency-security checks, formatting and production build passed.\\n- Browser: 86 development checks passed, with one production-only check skipped; 46 selected production checks passed, including the shortcuts screen in both themes.\\n- Global search: matching, owner navigation, pagination, invalid cursors, cancellation, stale responses and a 100,000-record benchmark were checked.\\n- Screenshot capture: 13 actual-interface images with capture provenance and no workload execution.\\n\\nFrozen source tags: [daemon and documentation](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow/tree/v1.0.14) and [Desktop](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow-desktop/tree/v1.0.14). [Full release tour](https://akoflow.com/blog/akoflow-1-0-14/)."},{"id":"akoflow-1-0-13","metadata":{"permalink":"/akoflow/blog/akoflow-1-0-13","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-05-akoflow-1-0-13.mdx","title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.13 \u2014 workflow monitoring, files, logs and temporal events","description":"Explore workflow outputs by producer, read complete and activity logs, and inspect temporal execution events.","date":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Desktop","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/desktop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Experiments","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/experiments"}],"readingTime":3.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Wesley Ferreira","title":"@ovvesley","url":"https://github.com/ovvesley","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12123871?v=4&size=96","key":"ovvesley","page":null},{"name":"Boosted with LLMs","key":"llms","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"akoflow-1-0-13","authors":["ovvesley","llms"],"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.13 \u2014 workflow monitoring, files, logs and temporal events","description":"Explore workflow outputs by producer, read complete and activity logs, and inspect temporal execution events.","image":"/img/releases/v1.0.13/01-workflow-overview.png","tags":["Desktop","Experiments"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.14 \u2014 global search, audit and infrastructure navigation","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/akoflow-1-0-14"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.12 brings MCP to Claude and Codex","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/create-workflows-with-claude-and-codex"}},"content":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.13 reorganizes execution monitoring around the workflow, its activities and the files they produce. This page describes changes since 1.0.12.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe screenshots below show the release candidate interface reading an existing completed six-activity Python workflow. Capturing them did not start a new execution. They demonstrate recorded observations, not a live filesystem listing. Capture provenance is stored alongside the images.\\n\\n## Workflow overview\\n\\nFollow the workflow as a whole: preparation artifacts, execution environments, recorded results and the execution plan. The unused external-input card has been removed.\\n\\n![Workflow overview](/img/releases/v1.0.13/01-workflow-overview.png)\\n\\n## Files next to their producer\\n\\nWorkspace now connects activities to generated file nodes. Each activity previews its five largest files; the \u201c\u2026\u201d node opens the complete list. Search activities or file paths in the explorer and click a result to focus the DAG. All, Path to activity, Branch, Fit view and fullscreen help navigate larger graphs.\\n\\n![Files next to their producer](/img/releases/v1.0.13/02-workspace-files.png)\\n\\n## Plan versus execution\\n\\nCompare planned and observed duration and cost, including absolute and relative differences. Activity counts and configured deadline/budget constraints remain explicit. Unconfigured constraints are not presented as satisfied SLAs. The Gantt charts retain their existing layout.\\n\\n![Plan versus execution](/img/releases/v1.0.13/03-plan-comparison.png)\\n\\n## Search activities while reading logs\\n\\nA searchable activity explorer focuses the log DAG. The main log remains the complete run output.\\n\\n![Search activities while reading logs](/img/releases/v1.0.13/04-log-navigation.png)\\n\\n## Complete run logs\\n\\nSearch messages and sources, navigate matches, wrap lines, follow new output, copy visible output, download logs or use fullscreen. Raw output without recorded timestamps is not given invented timestamps.\\n\\n![Complete run logs](/img/releases/v1.0.13/05-complete-logs.png)\\n\\n## Activity logs in a drawer\\n\\nClick an activity to open its log in a side drawer without replacing the complete run log. View activity opens its execution details.\\n\\n![Activity logs in a drawer](/img/releases/v1.0.13/06-activity-log-drawer.png)\\n\\n## Activity details\\n\\nThe activity drawer includes execution placement, timing, preparation and generated files, with a link to the owned activity page.\\n\\n![Activity details](/img/releases/v1.0.13/07-activity-details.png)\\n\\n## Preparation belongs to the activity\\n\\nPreparation is collapsible and updates during execution, with waiting, preparing, ready and failure states. Copy log reports clipboard success or failure. The separate run-wide Prerequisites tab has been removed.\\n\\n![Preparation belongs to the activity](/img/releases/v1.0.13/08-activity-preparation.png)\\n\\n## Generated files in activity details\\n\\nBoth the activity page and drawer reuse the Workspace file component, filtered by producer. Inspect size, checksum and recorded locations. Deleted manifest entries are excluded; recorded locations may no longer exist after workspace cleanup.\\n\\n![Generated files in activity details](/img/releases/v1.0.13/09-generated-files.png)\\n\\n## Temporal events\\n\\nWorkflow and activity lanes place events at recorded times. Event types use distinct marker shapes, and search/type filters narrow the view. Use the selector for overlapping or untimed events and download the complete available event collection as JSON.\\n\\n![Temporal events](/img/releases/v1.0.13/10-event-timeline.png)\\n\\n## Event details and phase observations\\n\\nClick a marker to inspect its data in a side drawer. Reconstructed timing observations carry an explicit source, duration and recorded timestamps; they are derived observations, not additional persisted audit events.\\n\\n![Event details and phase observations](/img/releases/v1.0.13/11-phase-event-details.png)\\n\\n## Timing totals with explanations\\n\\nExecution-time decomposition keeps aggregate totals and explains each phase. The duplicate per-activity phase table has moved into Events. Accumulated work can exceed elapsed wall time because activities overlap.\\n\\n![Timing totals with explanations](/img/releases/v1.0.13/12-time-decomposition.png)\\n\\n## Appearance and readability\\n\\nDesktop is the default interface style, with Simple available in Settings. Neutral actions, opaque surfaces, compact navigation and corrected selected-item contrast improve light/dark readability. Desktop appearance preferences survive local-server port changes and application restarts.\\n\\n![Appearance and readability](/img/releases/v1.0.13/13-dark-comparison.png)\\n\\n## Other changes since 1.0.12\\n\\n- Workflow shell commands can be checked for POSIX shell syntax through the daemon validation endpoint before execution. Validation does not execute the command.\\n- MCP activation no longer requires a detected native AI client; manual connection configuration remains available. MCP itself was introduced in 1.0.12.\\n- Research tooling is archived outside runtime packages, and downloadable scheduling snippets are isolated from daemon packages.\\n- Documentation now includes the MCP hands-on workflow tutorial and generated public release history.\\n\\n## Install and update\\n\\nGet the [1.0.13 release packages](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow/releases/tag/v1.0.13) or follow the [installation guide](/docs/downloads). Desktop packages are available for macOS universal, Windows x64 and Linux x64. Matching daemon and BuildKit runtime archives support Linux amd64 and arm64. Docker remains required.\\n\\nSee [execution monitoring](/docs/guides/workflows/executions) for the updated navigation and interpretation of timing observations."},{"id":"create-workflows-with-claude-and-codex","metadata":{"permalink":"/akoflow/blog/create-workflows-with-claude-and-codex","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-04-create-workflows-with-claude-and-codex.mdx","title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.12 brings MCP to Claude and Codex","description":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.12 introduces MCP integration. Connect Claude or Codex to create, execute and inspect scientific workflows, with a hands-on example.","date":"2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Getting started","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/getting-started"},{"inline":true,"label":"Desktop","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/desktop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Experiments","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/experiments"}],"readingTime":12.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Wesley Ferreira","title":"@ovvesley","url":"https://github.com/ovvesley","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12123871?v=4&size=96","key":"ovvesley","page":null},{"name":"Boosted with LLMs","key":"llms","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"create-workflows-with-claude-and-codex","authors":["ovvesley","llms"],"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.12 brings MCP to Claude and Codex","description":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.12 introduces MCP integration. Connect Claude or Codex to create, execute and inspect scientific workflows, with a hands-on example.","image":"/img/blog/mcp-first-workflow/hero-v2.png","cover_alt":"ChatGPT and Claude Code connected through MCP to Ak\xf4Flow and scientific workflows in a monochrome illustration.","cover_dag":["prepare","analyze","write-report"],"tags":["Getting started","Desktop","Experiments"],"hide_table_of_contents":false},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.13 \u2014 workflow monitoring, files, logs and temporal events","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/akoflow-1-0-13"},"nextItem":{"title":"From a DAG to your first Ak\xf4Flow run","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/from-a-dag-to-your-first-run"}},"content":"import WorkflowDag from \\"@site/src/components/WorkflowDag\\";\\n\\n**Starting with version 1.0.12, Ak\xf4Flow includes MCP integration to connect AI apps such as Claude and Codex directly to its workflow engine.** You can now describe an experiment in a conversation and ask your assistant to create workflow definitions, prepare execution plans, start runs and inspect their results through Ak\xf4Flow tools.\\n\\nMCP\u2014Model Context Protocol\u2014provides that connection. The assistant works with the same persisted definitions, plans and runs that you see in Desktop, using the permissions you grant to its connection. The [1.0.12 release](/docs/changelog) brings managed app connections, native client setup, connection verification, credential rotation and revocation.\\n\\nThis tutorial takes that connection from setup to a result you can inspect: three activities turn the measurements **2, 4 and 6** into a report containing **`Mean: 4.00`**. You will connect your AI app, give it focused prompts, inspect the saved DAG, and execute the workflow on a local Docker runtime.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What you need\\n\\n- [Ak\xf4Flow Desktop and its matching runtime](/docs/installation), open and showing **Connected**.\\n- Docker running on the machine that executes the activities.\\n- A local Codex client, Claude Code, or a supported Claude Desktop installation.\\n- An internet connection for the first BusyBox image download.\\n\\nThe setup screenshots below come from real, isolated homeserver Desktop captures. The 1.0.12 setup verifies the complete administration catalog; this tutorial only needs **Operate workflows**. Your connection paths, tool count and instance names will differ. The workflow and execution screenshots show the separate homeserver demonstration made for this post.\\n\\nThe native setup and connector were tested with real MCP clients. These are Ak\xf4Flow screenshots, not screenshots of an authenticated Claude or Codex conversation. The prompts are instructions for you to run in your own AI app; wording and approval dialogs can vary between clients.\\n\\n## 1. Enable MCP and choose an app\\n\\nOpen **Settings \u2192 MCP & AI apps**, then select **Enable MCP**.\\n\\n![MCP settings with the service disabled before activation](/img/releases/v1.0.12/01-mcp-disabled.png)\\n\\nSelect **Add app**, choose your client and give the connection a recognizable name, such as `Codex \u2014 workflow learning`. Select **Operate workflows**, then **Create connection**.\\n\\n![The app selector, connection name and permission choices in Ak\xf4Flow Desktop](/img/releases/v1.0.12/02-mcp-permissions.png)\\n\\nThe reference screenshot has **Full administration** selected to verify its complete catalog. For this walkthrough, select **Operate workflows** instead. **Read only** can inspect records but cannot create the workflow. Create a separate connection for each AI app so you can revoke one independently.\\n\\n## 2. Install the generated client configuration\\n\\nAk\xf4Flow generates a local **stdio** connection. The AI app starts the bundled connector on the same computer as Desktop. Keep Desktop open while using it.\\n\\n![The generated Codex configuration and its native installation action](/img/releases/v1.0.12/03-mcp-configuration.png)\\n\\nUse the entry generated by your installation. The executable, helper and private connection-file paths in these screenshots belong to the documentation machine. Do not copy those paths into your own configuration.\\n\\n### Codex\\n\\n1. Select **Add to Codex** in the setup dialog.\\n2. Fully quit and reopen the app, then start a **new local chat**. For Codex CLI, start a new session.\\n3. In the CLI, use `/mcp` to check active servers. `codex mcp list` lists configured servers.\\n\\nAk\xf4Flow writes its entry into the local Codex configuration, preserving other servers. If you use a custom configuration location, use **Copy configuration** or **Open config** to merge the generated entry into the configuration your client actually loads. Codex supports local stdio servers through `config.toml`; see the [official Codex MCP documentation](https://developers.openai.com/codex/mcp).\\n\\n### Claude Code\\n\\n1. Choose **Claude Code** when creating the connection.\\n2. Select **Add to Claude Code** in its setup dialog.\\n3. Close the current Claude Code session and start a new one on this computer.\\n4. Use `/mcp` to inspect the connection.\\n\\nAk\xf4Flow installs a user-level stdio entry in `~/.claude.json`, preserving existing settings. For custom setups, use the generated entry and the [official Claude Code MCP guide](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp).\\n\\n### Claude Desktop\\n\\nOn macOS or Windows, choose **Claude Desktop** and select **Add to Claude Desktop**. For manual setup, use **Copy configuration**, open **Settings \u2192 Developer \u2192 Edit Config** in Claude Desktop, and merge the generated server into `mcpServers`. Preserve other entries. Fully quit and reopen Claude Desktop, then begin a new conversation.\\n\\n![Claude Desktop configuration in the Linux reference installation, showing the manual copy flow](/img/interface/mcp/08-desktop-claude-configuration.png)\\n\\nThis Linux reference capture shows the copy-only fallback. Use the installation method that your Ak\xf4Flow setup dialog offers. The [local MCP setup guide](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers) explains Claude Desktop\'s configuration and restart flow.\\n\\n## 3. Verify both ends of the connection\\n\\nIn Ak\xf4Flow, select **Test MCP connection**. A successful test authenticates a real MCP client, discovers the tools permitted for this connection, and reads the daemon identity.\\n\\n![A successful native MCP connection test in Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.12](/img/releases/v1.0.12/04-mcp-verified.png)\\n\\nNow send this prompt in your AI app:\\n\\n```text\\nUse the Ak\xf4Flow MCP tools to identify my connected Ak\xf4Flow instance\\nand list my workflows and available environments. If a list is empty,\\ntell me. Do not create or modify anything.\\n```\\n\\n**Checkpoint:** the assistant makes an Ak\xf4Flow tool call and returns records from your instance, or an explicit empty list. An answer saying \u201cI can help with Ak\xf4Flow\u201d does not verify the connection. If it has no tools, reconnect its MCP server or restart the AI app.\\n\\nThe native test verifies the service. This first request verifies that **your AI app** loaded the server.\\n\\n## 4. Ask for a concrete workflow\\n\\nStart with a small experiment whose answer you know. Send:\\n\\n```text\\nUse Ak\xf4Flow MCP to create a workflow named mcp-first-analysis in\\nnamespace learning. First check whether that name already exists;\\nif it does, show it and do not overwrite it.\\n\\nUse docker.io/library/busybox:1.36 with OCI delivery and sh -c.\\nCreate three activities, each limited to 0.1 CPU and 32Mi memory:\\n\\n1. prepare: write the values 2, 4 and 6, one per line, to measurements.txt.\\n2. analyze: depend on prepare, read measurements.txt, and use awk to\\n   write the arithmetic mean with two decimal places to mean.txt.\\n3. write-report: depend on analyze and write Mean: 4.00 to report.txt,\\n   deriving the value from mean.txt rather than hardcoding the answer.\\n\\nPrint each activity\'s output in its log and declare its output file\\nin expectedOutputs. Show the definition, create it through the MCP\\ntool, then read it back and return its workflow and version IDs.\\nDo not create a plan or start execution yet.\\n```\\n\\n<WorkflowDag\\n  showCaption={false}\\n  title=\\"MCP first analysis\\"\\n  stages={[\\"prepare\\", \\"analyze\\", \\"write-report\\"]}\\n/>\\n\\n| Activity       | Depends on | Output                      |\\n| -------------- | ---------- | --------------------------- |\\n| `prepare`      | \u2014          | `measurements.txt`: 2, 4, 6 |\\n| `analyze`      | `prepare`  | `mean.txt`: `4.00`          |\\n| `write-report` | `analyze`  | `report.txt`: `Mean: 4.00`  |\\n\\nOpen **Workflows \u2192 Definitions \u2192 mcp-first-analysis** in Desktop. The saved workflow must have three activities and two dependency arrows.\\n\\n![The three-activity workflow created through MCP in the homeserver demonstration](/img/blog/mcp-first-workflow/workflow.png)\\n\\n**Checkpoint:** compare the saved definition with the table. A chat response containing YAML is only a draft until the creation tool succeeds and readback confirms the record. Creating the definition does not execute it.\\n\\nFor an exact reference, [download the complete workflow YAML](/downloads/blog/mcp-first-workflow/mcp-first-analysis.yaml). It uses `command.executable.source` for the container image, `dependsOn` for ordering, and `expectedOutputs` for required files. Ak\xf4Flow stages a predecessor\'s workspace before its dependent activity starts. Each activity writes a different output filename.\\n\\n## 5. Select where it will run\\n\\nIf you have no local environment yet, follow [Connect your local machine and create a scope](/blog/from-a-dag-to-your-first-run). Test the connection before saving the environment and confirm it is online. An execution scope selects the environment versions offered to planning.\\n\\nAsk the assistant to discover those records:\\n\\n```text\\nUse Ak\xf4Flow MCP to inspect mcp-first-analysis and list execution scopes,\\ntheir environments, resources, runtime bindings and connection health.\\nFind a scope with a healthy local execution runtime that supports OCI\\ncontainers. Return the exact persisted IDs and explain the selection.\\nDo not provision cloud resources or execute anything. If no suitable\\nscope exists, tell me what I need to configure in Desktop.\\n```\\n\\nThen request the plan, replacing the bracketed text with the scope you reviewed:\\n\\n```text\\nCreate a manual plan for the saved version of mcp-first-analysis using\\n[my reviewed local scope]. Resolve IDs from the persisted records.\\nAssign all three activities to its local resource and execution runtime.\\nUse one second per activity as a planning estimate, preserve dependency\\norder, and save the plan through MCP. Read it back and return its ID,\\nassignments, runtime selection and execution mode. Do not execute yet.\\n```\\n\\nReview the saved plan in Desktop. **One second is an estimate**, not a measured duration or timeout. The first image download may take longer. The homeserver demonstration used one local Docker resource; it did not allocate cloud machines or use simulation.\\n\\n## 6. Execute once and wait for evidence\\n\\nWhen the plan matches your intent, send:\\n\\n```text\\nStart one real execution of the reviewed plan for mcp-first-analysis.\\nUse a unique run ID and return it as soon as submission succeeds.\\nPoll that run until it reaches a terminal state. Do not resubmit it\\nbecause it is pending or running. If it fails, inspect its activity\\nerrors and report the cause before creating any replacement run.\\n```\\n\\nOpen **Workflows \u2192 Definitions \u2192 mcp-first-analysis \u2192 Plans**, open the selected plan, and open its execution. Wait for **completed** and **3/3** completed activities.\\n\\n![The real homeserver execution completed all three activities](/img/blog/mcp-first-workflow/completed-run.png)\\n\\nMCP submissions can return an operation or pending receipt before the work finishes. Submission success is not execution success. Keep the returned identifier and inspect that same operation or run instead of starting duplicates.\\n\\n## 7. Read the result, not just the status\\n\\n```text\\nInspect the completed run using Ak\xf4Flow MCP. Report each activity\'s\\nstatus and exit code, inspect the final activity output and generated\\nfiles, and verify that report.txt contains Mean: 4.00. Include file\\nchecksums when available and the workflow, plan and run IDs.\\nDistinguish files listed in metadata from file contents actually read.\\nIf content cannot be retrieved, say so instead of inferring it.\\n```\\n\\nIn Desktop, open the run\'s **Activities**, select **write-report**, and open its activity detail page. Inspect **Activity output** and **Generated files**.\\n\\n![The final activity output and generated files in the real demonstration](/img/blog/mcp-first-workflow/activity-output.png)\\n\\n### Follow the data through the workflow\\n\\nOpen the run\u2019s **Workspaces & data** tab. The activity/file graph connects each producer to its recorded output: `prepare` produces `measurements.txt`, `analyze` produces `mean.txt`, and `write-report` produces `report.txt`.\\n\\n![The real run\u2019s data graph links each activity to the file it generated](/img/blog/mcp-first-workflow/run-data.png)\\n\\nOpen **prepare** and scroll to **Activity output** to check the actual input values. The log prints `2`, `4` and `6`; the generated-file record identifies `measurements.txt` and its checksum.\\n\\n![The prepare activity prints the input measurements and records measurements.txt with its checksum](/img/blog/mcp-first-workflow/prepare-data.png)\\n\\nOpen **analyze** to inspect the intermediate result. Its output is `4.00`, and its generated file is `mean.txt`.\\n\\n![The analyze activity prints 4.00 and records mean.txt with its checksum](/img/blog/mcp-first-workflow/analyze-data.png)\\n\\nBack in **Workspaces & data**, scroll to the recorded file locations and **Data transfers**. The demonstration records two workspace transfers totaling **17 bytes**. These transfers stage predecessor workspaces, so the second transfer includes the intermediate workspace\u2019s files; its byte count is not just the size of `mean.txt`. Recorded locations describe execution evidence and may become unavailable after ephemeral workspaces are released.\\n\\n![Recorded file locations and the two data transfers between the workflow activities](/img/blog/mcp-first-workflow/data-transfers.png)\\n\\nThe demonstration created `mcp-first-analysis`, saved `mcp-analysis-plan`, and executed `mcp-analysis-run`. The real run completed all three activities. Reading the final workspace file confirmed:\\n\\n```text\\nMean: 4.00\\n```\\n\\nThe workflow creation, planning, submission and status retrieval went through a real stdio MCP client on the homeserver. The final file contents were additionally checked directly in the isolated runtime workspace. [Download the observed verification record](/downloads/blog/mcp-first-workflow/observed-results.json).\\n\\n## More prompts to try\\n\\nChange one aspect at a time so you can explain the result.\\n\\n**A new dataset:**\\n\\n```text\\nCreate a new workflow named mcp-analysis-10-20-30 using the same\\nthree-stage structure, but with values 10, 20 and 30. Preserve the\\noriginal workflow. Show the changed commands and expected answer\\nbefore saving the new definition. Do not execute yet.\\n```\\n\\n**Independent branches:**\\n\\n```text\\nDraft a workflow that prepares measurements, computes the mean and\\nmaximum in independent activities, then joins both into one report.\\nShow its DAG and file handoffs. Add dependencies only where a stage\\nneeds its predecessor\'s files, and use distinct output filenames.\\nExplain whether my selected resource capacity permits parallel work.\\nDo not save or execute until I review the draft.\\n```\\n\\n**Investigate a failure:**\\n\\n```text\\nInspect run [run ID] through Ak\xf4Flow MCP. Find the first failing\\nactivity, quote the relevant error briefly, and distinguish an image,\\nconnection, planning or command failure. Propose the smallest fix.\\nDo not change infrastructure or retry the run yet.\\n```\\n\\n**Prepare a reproducibility summary:**\\n\\n```text\\nSummarize run [run ID] using persisted Ak\xf4Flow records: workflow version,\\ncontainer reference and resolved digest if recorded, scope, assignments,\\nactivity outcomes, output paths and available checksums. Mark missing\\nevidence explicitly. Do not treat planned durations as measurements.\\n```\\n\\n## When a step does not work\\n\\n| Symptom                                                             | Next check                                                                         |\\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| Ak\xf4Flow verifies MCP but the assistant has no tools                 | Restart the client, start a new local session, and inspect its active MCP servers. |\\n| The assistant can list records but cannot create them               | Recreate its connection with **Operate workflows**.                                |\\n| Configuration points to another computer or an old application path | Generate it again from the Desktop installation on the client machine.             |\\n| No healthy local runtime appears                                    | Test the environment connection and inspect its resource/runtime binding.          |\\n| A run is pending                                                    | Inspect its operation and run events; keep polling the same ID.                    |\\n| The first activity cannot start                                     | Inspect its error and Docker/image availability before retrying.                   |\\n| `analyze` cannot find `measurements.txt`                            | Check the dependency, predecessor outcome and workspace-staging evidence.          |\\n| The run completes but the answer differs                            | Read the saved commands and actual files; verify the input values and formatting.  |\\n\\nUse **Disable MCP** to pause connections, **Rotate credential** to replace a connection\'s credential, or **Revoke access** to remove one app\'s access. Reconnect the AI app after rotation. Keep secret values out of prompts and use the generated private references.\\n\\nYou now have a reusable pattern: describe a checkable experiment, save and inspect its definition, select a compatible execution scope, review a plan, execute once, and inspect the evidence. Continue with [workflow authoring](/docs/guides/workflows/definitions), [planning](/docs/guides/workflows/planning), and the [MCP setup and permissions guide](/docs/guides/operations/mcp-ai-apps)."},{"id":"from-a-dag-to-your-first-run","metadata":{"permalink":"/akoflow/blog/from-a-dag-to-your-first-run","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-21-from-a-dag-to-your-first-run.mdx","title":"From a DAG to your first Ak\xf4Flow run","description":"With Ak\xf4Flow Desktop installed, follow annotated screenshots to create your first local workflow, execute it, and inspect its output.","date":"2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Getting started","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/getting-started"},{"inline":true,"label":"Desktop","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/desktop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Experiments","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/experiments"}],"readingTime":9.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Wesley Ferreira","title":"@ovvesley","url":"https://github.com/ovvesley","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12123871?v=4&size=96","key":"ovvesley","page":null},{"name":"Boosted with LLMs","key":"llms","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"from-a-dag-to-your-first-run","authors":["ovvesley","llms"],"title":"From a DAG to your first Ak\xf4Flow run","description":"With Ak\xf4Flow Desktop installed, follow annotated screenshots to create your first local workflow, execute it, and inspect its output.","image":"/img/interface/workflows/definitions.png","cover_alt":"Ak\xf4Flow Desktop workflow definitions catalog with the Import YAML action.","cover_dag":["write-report"],"tags":["Getting started","Desktop","Experiments"],"hide_table_of_contents":false},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ak\xf4Flow 1.0.12 brings MCP to Claude and Codex","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/create-workflows-with-claude-and-codex"},"nextItem":{"title":"Montage 050: a sky made of workflows","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/montage-050-from-observations-to-a-mosaic"}},"content":"import WorkflowDag from \\"@site/src/components/WorkflowDag\\";\\nimport AnnotatedScreenshot from \\"@site/src/components/blog/AnnotatedScreenshot\\";\\nimport tutorialShots from \\"@site/src/components/blog/tutorialShots\\";\\n\\nWith Ak\xf4Flow Desktop already installed, your first experiment can start on your own workstation. Describe one activity in YAML, select your local machine, and watch a real execution produce a file. Then turn that first activity into a small analysis pipeline.\\n\\nThis walkthrough uses **Ak\xf4Flow Desktop throughout**. Desktop starts its matching local services and manages the connection to the engine. You do not need to clone the source repository, start a development Compose stack, or copy an API token.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What you will build\\n\\nBegin with `write-report`: one activity that writes `result.txt` and prints its contents in the activity log. This checks the complete path from a definition to a local execution and its recorded output.\\n\\nAfter that succeeds, import a second YAML with three activities: prepare three measurements, compute their mean, and write a report. The expected answer is **4.00**.\\n\\nBoth examples use a small BusyBox container on your local Docker engine. They perform **real execution**. They do not simulate work or allocate cloud machines.\\n\\n## 1. Begin after Desktop is installed\\n\\nThis walkthrough starts **after installing Ak\xf4Flow Desktop**. If you still need the application, follow [Install Ak\xf4Flow Desktop](/docs/installation), then return here.\\n\\n1. Start Docker and open the installed Ak\xf4Flow Desktop application.\\n2. Let the engine checkup finish and wait for **Overview**.\\n3. Confirm **Connected** at the bottom-left corner.\\n4. Start with **Infrastructure \u2192 Environments** in the sidebar.\\n\\nThe screenshots below were captured from the actual interface. They show the controls using an existing reference instance: its workflow is named `hello`, its activity is `a`, and its local environment is `Local environment`. **For your experiment, enter the tutorial names `first-local-check`, `write-report`, `Local check`, and `First local scope`.** Your fresh catalogs may be empty. The reference run\'s counts and files are identified in each caption rather than presented as your experiment\'s results.\\n\\nEach red number has a matching instruction below the image. **Click a screenshot to enlarge it with its arrows**; on a phone, scroll the enlarged view sideways to read the controls.\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.connected} />\\n\\n**Checkpoint:** Desktop is connected. If startup fails, open its failure details before retrying.\\n\\n## 2. Connect your local machine\\n\\nOpen **Infrastructure \u2192 Environments**. To create the environment from scratch, follow the two screens below. If you already created `Local check`, open it and continue with its connection check instead.\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.environments} />\\n\\n1. Select **Connect environment** (called **New environment** in some releases), then **Local machine**.\\n2. Name it `Local check`.\\n3. Select **Test connection**.\\n4. When the test succeeds, select **Save environment**.\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.localForm} />\\n\\nOn the environment page, select **Check now**. Wait until **Local machine** shows **online**. This makes the local resource available for planning.\\n\\nAn _environment_ describes where the work can run. Here, the target is the machine reached through your local Ak\xf4Flow engine. Desktop requests connection checks from the daemon.\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.checkOnline} />\\n\\n**Checkpoint:** the local resource is online. Remember its environment name for step 5.\\n\\n## 3. Save your first workflow YAML\\n\\nCreate a plain-text file named `first-local-check.yaml`, or [download the complete YAML](/downloads/blog/first-local-run/first-local-check.yaml). Copy the whole document, preserving indentation, and save it with the `.yaml` extension.\\n\\n```yaml\\nname: first-local-check\\nspec:\\n  namespace: learning\\n  activities:\\n    - name: write-report\\n      cpuLimit: \\"0.1\\"\\n      memoryLimit: 32Mi\\n      command:\\n        executable:\\n          source:\\n            type: oci\\n            reference: docker.io/library/busybox:1.36\\n          delivery:\\n            strategy: auto\\n            targetFormat: oci\\n        entrypoint: sh\\n        arguments:\\n          - -c\\n          - |\\n            printf \'Akoflow local check\\\\n\' > result.txt\\n            cat result.txt\\n      expectedOutputs: [result.txt]\\n```\\n\\nRead the document from the outside in:\\n\\n- `name` identifies the workflow. `namespace` groups it under `learning`.\\n- `activities` contains one unit of work: `write-report`.\\n- `command.executable` names the BusyBox image. `targetFormat: oci` selects the container format used by local Docker.\\n- `entrypoint: sh` and the `-c` argument run the following shell script.\\n- `printf` creates the file in the activity workspace. `cat` prints it into the activity log.\\n- `expectedOutputs` asserts that `result.txt` exists after execution.\\n- CPU and memory limits describe the activity\'s small resource demand.\\n\\nThe image comes from Docker Hub, so its first use may take longer while it downloads. For a research reproducibility bundle, also retain the resolved image digest alongside the version tag.\\n\\n## 4. Import the YAML in Desktop\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.import} />\\n\\n1. Open **Workflows \u2192 Definitions**.\\n2. Select **Import YAML** and choose `first-local-check.yaml`.\\n3. Open the new `first-local-check` definition.\\n4. Confirm that the DAG contains one activity named **write-report**.\\n\\nA _DAG_ is a directed acyclic graph: nodes are activities and arrows are dependencies. This first graph has one node and no arrows, enough to learn the execution lifecycle before adding data handoffs.\\n\\nIf the name already exists, open the existing definition or change `name` before importing another copy. The importer normalizes names into identifiers, so each new definition needs a distinct name.\\n\\n**Checkpoint:** the definition exists and its graph matches the YAML. Importing saves a definition; it does not start a run.\\n\\n## 5. Create a local execution scope\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.scopeCatalog} />\\n\\n1. Open **Infrastructure \u2192 Execution scopes**.\\n2. Select **Create scope** (called **New execution scope** in some releases).\\n3. Name it `First local scope` and select your local environment.\\n4. Leave **Create the initial network topology** enabled.\\n5. Select **Create execution scope**.\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.scopeName} />\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.scopeEnvironment} />\\n\\nAfter selecting `Local check`, scroll to the bottom of the form to save it:\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.scopeSave} />\\n\\nA _scope_ selects the environment resources offered to planning. Its topology describes the connections between them. A one-machine experiment needs no network links.\\n\\nDesktop creates these records and their identifiers. The YAML here describes the workflow; you do not need to author environment, scope, or plan API envelopes.\\n\\n## 6. Make a plan and start the run\\n\\nReturn to `first-local-check` and select **Generate plan**:\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.planAction} />\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.manualMode} />\\n\\n1. Choose **Create manually**.\\n2. Select `First local scope`.\\n3. Select **write-report** in the activity graph.\\n4. Choose your **local machine runtime** and **local machine resource**.\\n5. Set **Expected execution time** to `1` second.\\n6. Select **Generate plan**.\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.placement} />\\n\\nThe duration is an estimate, not a timeout or a measurement. Image download and startup can make the first run take longer.\\n\\nOn the saved plan page, select **Execute plan**:\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.execute} />\\n\\nReview the selected plan, **Real execution**, and the local runtime. Keep the suggested unique run ID or enter your own:\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.review} />\\n\\nScroll to the bottom, then select **Start execution** once:\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.start} />\\n\\nWait until the run says **completed** and **1/1** activities settled:\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.completed} />\\n\\nA _plan_ describes where work should run. A _run_ records what happened. Submission is asynchronous: pending or running can appear before completed.\\n\\n## 7. Inspect your first result\\n\\nOn the completed run, select **Activities**, then `write-report`. Open its **Details** link or **Open activity page** from its information panel (some releases label this **Open activity details**).\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.activity} />\\n\\n| Where to look       | Expected result                       |\\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\\n| Execution details   | **Exit code 0**                       |\\n| Activity output/log | `Akoflow local check`                 |\\n| **Generated files** | `result.txt`, with a SHA-256 checksum |\\n\\nScroll down the activity page to **Activity output** and **Generated files**:\\n\\n<AnnotatedScreenshot shot={tutorialShots.files} />\\n\\nThe generated file belongs to the activity workspace managed by the engine. Listing a file records that it was observed; it does not automatically download it into your computer\'s Downloads folder.\\n\\nYou have now submitted and inspected a real local experiment. Save the YAML with your experiment material so the definition can be reused.\\n\\n## 8. Extend it into a small analysis\\n\\nSave the next document as `first-local-analysis.yaml`, or [download the complete analysis YAML](/downloads/blog/first-local-run/first-local-analysis.yaml). This is a **new, complete workflow**, with a different name from the first definition.\\n\\n<WorkflowDag\\n  title=\\"First local analysis\\"\\n  stages={[\\"prepare\\", \\"analyze\\", \\"write-report\\"]}\\n/>\\n\\n| Activity       | Reads                                   | Produces                              |\\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\\n| `prepare`      | Values declared in its command: 2, 4, 6 | `measurements.txt`                    |\\n| `analyze`      | `measurements.txt`                      | `mean.txt`, containing `4.00`         |\\n| `write-report` | `mean.txt`                              | `report.txt`, containing `Mean: 4.00` |\\n\\n```yaml\\nname: first-local-analysis\\nspec:\\n  namespace: learning\\n  activities:\\n    - name: prepare\\n      cpuLimit: \\"0.1\\"\\n      memoryLimit: 32Mi\\n      command:\\n        executable:\\n          source:\\n            type: oci\\n            reference: docker.io/library/busybox:1.36\\n          delivery:\\n            strategy: auto\\n            targetFormat: oci\\n        entrypoint: sh\\n        arguments:\\n          - -c\\n          - |\\n            printf \'2\\\\n4\\\\n6\\\\n\' > measurements.txt\\n            cat measurements.txt\\n      expectedOutputs: [measurements.txt]\\n\\n    - name: analyze\\n      dependsOn: [prepare]\\n      cpuLimit: \\"0.1\\"\\n      memoryLimit: 32Mi\\n      command:\\n        executable:\\n          source:\\n            type: oci\\n            reference: docker.io/library/busybox:1.36\\n          delivery:\\n            strategy: auto\\n            targetFormat: oci\\n        entrypoint: sh\\n        arguments:\\n          - -c\\n          - |\\n            awk \'{sum += $1; n++} END {printf \\"%.2f\\\\n\\", sum/n}\' measurements.txt > mean.txt\\n            cat mean.txt\\n      expectedOutputs: [mean.txt]\\n\\n    - name: write-report\\n      dependsOn: [analyze]\\n      cpuLimit: \\"0.1\\"\\n      memoryLimit: 32Mi\\n      command:\\n        executable:\\n          source:\\n            type: oci\\n            reference: docker.io/library/busybox:1.36\\n          delivery:\\n            strategy: auto\\n            targetFormat: oci\\n        entrypoint: sh\\n        arguments:\\n          - -c\\n          - |\\n            printf \'Mean: %s\\\\n\' \\"$(cat mean.txt)\\" > report.txt\\n            cat report.txt\\n      expectedOutputs: [report.txt]\\n```\\n\\nThe new field is `dependsOn`. `analyze` waits for `prepare`, and `write-report` waits for `analyze`. Ak\xf4Flow stages the predecessor\'s workspace before a dependent activity starts. Each stage writes a different output path, avoiding conflicting contents at the same path. `expectedOutputs` verifies that the required file exists.\\n\\nRepeat the Desktop sequence:\\n\\n1. Import `first-local-analysis.yaml` in **Workflows \u2192 Definitions**.\\n2. Confirm the three-node DAG above.\\n3. Create a manual plan using the existing `First local scope`.\\n4. Assign **all three activities** to the local runtime and resource, with an expected duration of `1` second each.\\n5. Generate the plan, choose **Execute plan \u2192 Real execution**, and start it.\\n6. Wait for **completed** and **3/3** activities settled.\\n7. Open the final **write-report** activity. Its log should print `Mean: 4.00`. Its generated files should include `report.txt` with a checksum.\\n\\nThis graph is sequential because each stage consumes the previous stage\'s output. A future independent branch should declare only the dependencies it needs. Dependencies determine readiness; resource capacity determines whether ready activities can run together.\\n\\n## If something stops you\\n\\n| Symptom                                               | Next action                                                                                                |\\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| Desktop does not reach **Connected**                  | Check Docker and inspect first-start failure details.                                                      |\\n| Local environment is offline or missing from planning | Test its connection, save it, and select **Check now**.                                                    |\\n| YAML import fails                                     | Check indentation, retain the complete `name`/`spec` wrapper, and use a distinct workflow name.            |\\n| No compatible runtime appears                         | Recheck the environment\'s runtime binding and select a real local scope.                                   |\\n| The first activity cannot obtain its image            | Inspect the activity error and verify access to Docker Hub and the local Docker engine.                    |\\n| Analysis cannot find its input                        | Check `dependsOn`, the predecessor\'s completion, and workspace-staging evidence.                           |\\n| A run fails or stays pending                          | Open activity details and run events, then see [Troubleshooting](/docs/guides/operations/troubleshooting). |\\n\\n## The idea behind Ak\xf4Flow\\n\\nThe original Ak\xf4Flow paper introduces middleware for parallel scientific workflows in containerized environments, with provenance capture and an astronomy evaluation. It gives the research context for the project:\\n\\nFerreira, W.; Kunstmann, L.; Paes, A.; Bedo, M.; de Oliveira, D. **Ak\xf4Flow: um Middleware para execu\xe7\xe3o de Workflows cient\xedficos em m\xfaltiplos ambientes conteinerizados.** SBBD, 2024, pp. 27\u201339. [Read the paper](https://doi.org/10.5753/sbbd.2024.241126).\\n\\nThis post follows the current Desktop interface and portable workflow format, which have evolved since the publication. The paper explains the research motivation; the documentation and downloadable YAML files describe the steps used here.\\n\\nHere, the YAML is the workflow definition, the environment is your local machine, the scope selects eligible resources, the plan assigns activities, and the run holds observed results. Generated files and checksums provide a first piece of execution evidence.\\n\\nFor your next experiment, change the measurements in a new copy of the analysis YAML and check that the mean changes accordingly. Continue with [planning alternatives](/docs/guides/workflows/planning), [workflow authoring](/docs/guides/workflows/definitions), or the [Montage 050 scientific workflow](/docs/showcase/scientific-workflows/montage-050).\\n\\nAPI automation is a separate next step once you know these objects; see [API access](/docs/tutorials/api-access). The Desktop path manages its internal credentials for you."},{"id":"montage-050-from-observations-to-a-mosaic","metadata":{"permalink":"/akoflow/blog/montage-050-from-observations-to-a-mosaic","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-21-montage-050.mdx","title":"Montage 050: a sky made of workflows","description":"Twelve observations, 58 activities, one astronomical mosaic. Explore the science, reproduce the local workflow, and read the evidence behind a completed experiment.","date":"2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Scientific workflows","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/scientific-workflows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Astronomy","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/astronomy"},{"inline":true,"label":"Experiments","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/tags/experiments"}],"readingTime":18.27,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Wesley Ferreira","title":"@ovvesley","url":"https://github.com/ovvesley","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12123871?v=4&size=96","key":"ovvesley","page":null},{"name":"Boosted with LLMs","key":"llms","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"montage-050-from-observations-to-a-mosaic","authors":["ovvesley","llms"],"title":"Montage 050: a sky made of workflows","description":"Twelve observations, 58 activities, one astronomical mosaic. Explore the science, reproduce the local workflow, and read the evidence behind a completed experiment.","image":"/img/blog/montage-050/mosaic-color.png","cover_alt":"The 1800 by 1800 pixel color mosaic produced by the completed Ak\xf4Flow Montage hybrid experiment.","cover_style":"photograph","tags":["Scientific workflows","Astronomy","Experiments"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"From a DAG to your first Ak\xf4Flow run","permalink":"/akoflow/blog/from-a-dag-to-your-first-run"}},"content":"import WorkflowDag from \\"@site/src/components/WorkflowDag\\";\\n\\nThe stars in the opening image are the output of a scientific computation. Before the color mosaic existed, individual observations had to be reprojected, their overlaps compared, their backgrounds reconciled, and their pixels combined. Ak\xf4Flow coordinates those steps as a workflow and keeps the execution evidence alongside the result.\\n\\nThis field note follows **Montage 050**, a 58-activity astronomy workflow. We will look at what the activities do, how to reproduce the local example, and what an existing completed experiment tells us about planning and execution.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Start with the image\u2014and its provenance\\n\\nThe cover is the actual **1800 \xd7 1800** color preview from run `montage-docker-image-real-20261010-v2`. It is a recorded product of a completed hybrid GCP/HPC experiment. Click it to enlarge the image, or [open the original PNG](/img/blog/montage-050/mosaic-color.png).\\n\\nThat experiment and the local reproduction described below use different execution environments. The cover and historical timings establish what the completed hybrid run produced; they are not a claim that the two-slot local example finished in the same time.\\n\\nThe preserved PNG is 4,920,451 bytes. Its SHA-256 is:\\n\\n```text\\n63fa4d8447274bdb8a44f6be8cf5a23c0a2f0baa479bd2ae1ec3b541df4e19f2\\n```\\n\\nThe image copied into this site matches that checksum. You can download the [observed result summary](/downloads/blog/montage-050/observed-results.json) to connect the picture to the run, activity counts, timings, and audit checks.\\n\\n## What Montage does for astronomy\\n\\nA sky survey records many images rather than a single photograph of an entire region. Those images may use different pixel grids and have different background levels. Simply placing them next to each other would leave misalignment and visible seams.\\n\\n[Montage, developed at IPAC](https://www.ipac.caltech.edu/project/montage), combines astronomical images while preserving positional and calibration fidelity. Its building blocks operate on FITS files, the scientific image products in this workflow. The color PNG is a visualization of the resulting mosaics; retain the FITS products for scientific analysis.\\n\\nThree operations explain much of the computation:\\n\\n- **Reprojection:** map observations onto a common output grid. The [mProject documentation](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/mProject.html) describes the input image, header template, reprojected image, and accompanying area image.\\n- **Background matching:** measure overlap differences and solve for consistent corrections. [mBgModel](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/mBgModel.html) uses the fitted differences to estimate a global set of adjustments.\\n- **Coaddition:** combine corrected observations into mosaics. The [Montage module guide](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/how.html) describes the tools for metadata generation, reprojection, background correction, and mosaicking.\\n\\nHere, twelve input FITS observations feed three image-band branches. Each branch produces a FITS mosaic and a monochrome preview; a final activity combines the three mosaics into the color preview.\\n\\n## Which Montage example and which public data?\\n\\nThis example is **Montage 050**, preserved in the original image as `050d`. The identifier names the workload configuration; it does **not** mean that the graph contains 50 activities. Section 4.1 and Table 1 of the [Ak\xf4Flow paper, SBBD 2024](https://doi.org/10.5753/sbbd.2024.241126), describe the smaller \u201c0.5\u201d configuration with the same inventory used here: 12 projections, 18 overlap fits, and 58 activity executions in total. The paper also evaluates a larger \u201c1.0\u201d configuration with 472 executions. This walkthrough uses the smaller one. The bundled FITS header templates define its actual output footprint and pixel grid.\\n\\nThe inputs are **real, publicly accessible astronomical survey images**, rather than generated test pictures. Their names identify the **POSS2/UKSTU** survey products distributed through the [Digitized Sky Survey (DSS) archive at IRSA](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/data/DSS/). DSS digitizes photographic survey plates from the Palomar and UK Schmidt telescopes; IRSA provides image access and links to the originating STScI archive. The [IRSA dataset summary](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/docs/irsaviewer_datasets.html) documents that survey lineage. Public access does not remove the archive\'s attribution and copyright requirements; retain the DSS/STScI and original survey acknowledgments when reusing the images.\\n\\nThe pinned bundle contains four input tiles in each of three survey bands:\\n\\n| Branch | Source filename family   | Source band   | FITS mosaic     | Display channel in the final PNG |\\n| ------ | ------------------------ | ------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------- |\\n| `1`    | `poss2ukstu_blue_*.fits` | Blue          | `1-mosaic.fits` | Blue                             |\\n| `2`    | `poss2ukstu_red_*.fits`  | Red           | `2-mosaic.fits` | Green                            |\\n| `3`    | `poss2ukstu_ir_*.fits`   | Near infrared | `3-mosaic.fits` | Red                              |\\n\\nThat is **12 FITS inputs**, approximately 87 MiB in the image\'s input seed. The RGB assignment is a visualization choice: the red survey band is displayed in green, and the infrared band in red. Read the scientific FITS products and their headers when interpreting measurements; PNG colors alone do not identify an observing wavelength.\\n\\nThe exact command and file inventory is available in the [pinned `workflow.yaml`](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow-examples/blob/d890c3171e3661890305b763b8fc3ede48bb61b4/scientific-workflows/montage-050/workflow.yaml). Keeping that revision and the container digest identifies the packaged input set used for reproduction. Acquiring a fresh cutout from the public archive may select different pixels or preprocessing, so it is a new input set to record explicitly.\\n\\n## Inside the 58-activity graph\\n\\nThe local bundle preserves the original commands, activity names, and **138 named file handoffs**. Its activity inventory is:\\n\\n| Native command | Activities | Responsibility                                          |\\n| -------------- | ---------: | ------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `mProject`     |         12 | Reproject the input observations                        |\\n| `mDiffFit`     |         18 | Compare overlaps and fit their differences              |\\n| `mConcatFit`   |          3 | Assemble fit results for each band                      |\\n| `mBgModel`     |          3 | Solve each band\'s background corrections                |\\n| `mBackground`  |         12 | Apply corrections to the projected observations         |\\n| `mImgtbl`      |          3 | Build metadata tables for corrected images              |\\n| `mAdd`         |          3 | Coadd the three FITS mosaics                            |\\n| `mViewer`      |          4 | Produce three monochrome previews and one color preview |\\n| **Total**      |     **58** |                                                         |\\n\\nThe diagram below comes from the local example\'s activity and dependency contract. Enlarge it to follow individual edges.\\n\\n<WorkflowDag title=\\"Montage 050 \u2014 Astronomical Image Mosaicking\\" stages={[]} />\\n\\nIndependent projections and overlap operations can run concurrently after their own inputs are ready. Background modeling introduces a convergence point within each band. The final color preview waits for its three mosaic inputs. There is no extra global barrier forcing an unrelated band to wait at every stage.\\n\\nThis is why the DAG matters: it encodes scientific dependencies. A scheduler can choose where ready work runs, but cannot skip an input merely to make the graph appear more parallel. The [Montage parallelization guide](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/grid.html) explains the relationship between independent image operations and background-model convergence.\\n\\n## What each activity does, and what its parameters mean\\n\\nThe scientific design follows Jacob et al., [_Montage: a grid portal and software toolkit for science-grade astronomical image mosaicking_](https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.4454), especially its toolkit components and workflow discussion. Reprojection aligns the pixel grids; overlap fitting supplies evidence for background adjustments; corrected images are then coadded. The sections below combine that explanation with the module manuals and the **actual arguments in this example**.\\n\\nAn activity type, such as `mProject`, can have several executions in the graph. Here, the same processing chain repeats for three bands. The examples below show the first band unless noted otherwise. They run inside an activity workspace after Ak\xf4Flow has staged the declared inputs; copying one command into an empty directory is not a complete reproduction.\\n\\n### `mProject`: put one observation on the common sky grid\\n\\n```bash\\nmProject -X poss2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits pposs2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits region-oversized.hdr\\n```\\n\\nEach of the **12 executions** reprojects one observation. It maps input pixels through sky coordinates onto the requested output grid, accounting for pixel overlap rather than merely resizing a picture. Its outputs include the projected FITS image and an accompanying `_area.fits` coverage image.\\n\\n| Parameter                       | Meaning in this example                                                                                                                                                 |\\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `-X`                            | Expand the template region as necessary to retain the input image\'s extent.                                                                                             |\\n| `poss2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits`  | Input observation.                                                                                                                                                      |\\n| `pposs2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits` | Projected output; the `p` prefix is this workflow\'s naming convention.                                                                                                  |\\n| `region-oversized.hdr`          | FITS header template defining the working grid, coordinate projection, reference coordinates, and pixel scale. It is used before the final mosaic footprint is applied. |\\n\\nThese tasks have no predecessor activities and can start independently when execution slots are available. See the [official `mProject` manual](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/mProject.html).\\n\\n### `mDiffFit`: measure how two projected images disagree\\n\\n```bash\\nmDiffFit -d -s 1-fit.000001.000002.txt pposs2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits pposs2ukstu_blue_001_002.fits 1-diff.000001.000002.fits region-oversized.hdr\\n```\\n\\nThe **18 executions** compare overlapping pairs: six pairs among the four images in each band. `mDiffFit` combines image differencing (`mDiff`) with a plane fit (`mFitplane`). It measures relative background offsets and gradients that the next stages need.\\n\\n| Parameter                             | Meaning in this example                                               |\\n| ------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `-d`                                  | Enable diagnostic output.                                             |\\n| `-s 1-fit.000001.000002.txt`          | Write the structured fit/status result for this pair to a named file. |\\n| The two `pposs2ukstu_\u2026fits` arguments | Projected images to compare; both projection tasks must finish first. |\\n| `1-diff.000001.000002.fits`           | Difference-image path used by the combined operation.                 |\\n| `region-oversized.hdr`                | Shared working-grid template.                                         |\\n\\nDifferent pairs can run concurrently after their respective inputs arrive. See the [Montage grid-tool description](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/gridtools.html) and [`mDiffFit` implementation and command interface](https://github.com/Caltech-IPAC/Montage/blob/main/grid/Pegasus/mDiffFit.c).\\n\\n### `mConcatFit`: gather the pairwise measurements\\n\\n```bash\\nmConcatFit 1-stat.tbl 1-fits.tbl .\\n```\\n\\nThere are **three executions**, one per band. Each gathers the individual overlap-fit records into the table required by background modeling. It combines **fit parameters**, not image pixels.\\n\\n| Parameter    | Meaning in this example                                                    |\\n| ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `1-stat.tbl` | Input table identifying the fit-result files to collect.                   |\\n| `1-fits.tbl` | Output table of pairwise fit measurements.                                 |\\n| `.`          | Directory containing those staged fit-result files: the current workspace. |\\n\\nThis task waits for the band\'s six `mDiffFit` executions. The [grid-tool guide](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/gridtools.html) explains why independently generated fit records need this merge; the [`mConcatFit` source](https://github.com/Caltech-IPAC/Montage/blob/main/grid/Pegasus/mConcatFit.c) documents its three positional arguments.\\n\\n### `mBgModel`: solve a consistent background model for the band\\n\\n```bash\\nmBgModel -i 100000 1-images.tbl 1-fits.tbl 1-corrections.tbl\\n```\\n\\nThe **three executions** use the overlap measurements together to find per-image background corrections. A global solution within each band avoids treating each seam as an unrelated adjustment. The result is a correction table; the image pixels are changed by the following activity.\\n\\n| Parameter           | Meaning in this example                                                                                                                                          |\\n| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `-i 100000`         | Iteration count requested by this versioned workload\'s modeling command; it is not a timeout or CPU allocation. Preserve it when reproducing the pinned example. |\\n| `1-images.tbl`      | Input image metadata and identifiers.                                                                                                                            |\\n| `1-fits.tbl`        | Aggregated measurements from `mConcatFit`.                                                                                                                       |\\n| `1-corrections.tbl` | Output coefficients for correcting each image\'s background.                                                                                                      |\\n\\nThis is a convergence point for each band. Other bands can progress independently. The [official `mBgModel` manual](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/mBgModel.html) explains the solver and its arguments; documented iteration limits can differ from the packaged command, so the pinned image remains the reference for this reproduction.\\n\\n### `mBackground`: apply the correction to one projected image\\n\\n```bash\\nmBackground -t pposs2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits cposs2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits 1-projected.tbl 1-corrections.tbl\\n```\\n\\nEach of the **12 executions** subtracts the fitted background plane from one projected image. In the module\'s model, the plane has the form `A*x + B*y + C`. This matches relative backgrounds while retaining the image for coaddition; it is not a command to remove every astronomical source or all diffuse emission.\\n\\n| Parameter                       | Meaning in this example                                                                                                           |\\n| ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `-t`                            | Table mode: obtain the image\'s correction coefficients from the supplied tables rather than three explicit coefficient arguments. |\\n| `pposs2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits` | Projected input image.                                                                                                            |\\n| `cposs2ukstu_blue_001_001.fits` | Background-corrected output; `c` is the workflow\'s naming convention.                                                             |\\n| `1-projected.tbl`               | Metadata table used to identify the projected image.                                                                              |\\n| `1-corrections.tbl`             | Per-image corrections produced by `mBgModel`.                                                                                     |\\n\\nCorrections for different images can run concurrently once their band model and projected inputs are ready. See the [official `mBackground` manual](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/mBackground.html).\\n\\n### `mImgtbl`: describe the corrected files for the coaddition step\\n\\n```bash\\nmImgtbl . -t 1-corrected.tbl 1-updated-corrected.tbl\\n```\\n\\nThe **three executions** read FITS headers and create metadata tables. Those tables describe image geometry and file identity so that coaddition can place the corrected inputs correctly. This stage produces metadata, not another mosaic.\\n\\n| Parameter                 | Meaning in this example                                                                                   |\\n| ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `.`                       | Directory in which to read the corrected FITS files.                                                      |\\n| `-t 1-corrected.tbl`      | Restrict processing to the images named in this input list, rather than every FITS file in the workspace. |\\n| `1-updated-corrected.tbl` | Output table with the corrected files\' header metadata.                                                   |\\n\\nEach task waits for its band\'s four corrected images. The [official `mImgtbl` manual](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/mImgtbl.html) documents the table fields and selection option.\\n\\n### `mAdd`: coadd the corrected observations into one FITS mosaic\\n\\n```bash\\nmAdd -e 1-updated-corrected.tbl region.hdr 1-mosaic.fits\\n```\\n\\nThe **three executions** create one scientific mosaic per band from the corrected, aligned images. Coaddition combines contributions where pixels overlap; it is not concatenation of files or an RGB operation. Montage also produces an area/coverage mosaic.\\n\\n| Parameter                 | Meaning in this example                                                                                                         |\\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `-e`                      | Use the exact dimensions in the final header template instead of shrinking the mosaic to the data\'s bounding region.            |\\n| `1-updated-corrected.tbl` | Metadata table selecting the images to coadd.                                                                                   |\\n| `region.hdr`              | Final mosaic template, as distinct from the oversized working template used earlier. The recorded output is 1800 \xd7 1800 pixels. |\\n| `1-mosaic.fits`           | Output scientific mosaic for this band.                                                                                         |\\n\\nNo averaging-mode override (`-a`) is supplied in this command. The [official `mAdd` manual](https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Montage/docs/mAdd.html) describes the coaddition modes and area-image behavior.\\n\\n### `mViewer`: turn scientific mosaics into viewable PNGs\\n\\nThe first **three executions** render one preview per band:\\n\\n```bash\\nmViewer -ct 1 -gray 1-mosaic.fits -1s max gaussian -png 1-mosaic.png\\n```\\n\\n| Parameter             | Meaning in this example                                                                   |\\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `-ct 1`               | Select color-table index 1 for the single-band display.                                   |\\n| `-gray 1-mosaic.fits` | Read one FITS image for single-band rendering.                                            |\\n| `-1s max`             | Set the display range using a sigma-based lower bound and the maximum as the upper bound. |\\n| `gaussian`            | Use Gaussian histogram-based stretching for display.                                      |\\n| `-png 1-mosaic.png`   | Write the rendered PNG.                                                                   |\\n\\nThe **fourth execution** builds the color preview after all three `mAdd` tasks finish:\\n\\n```bash\\nmViewer -red 3-mosaic.fits -0.5s max gaussian-log -green 2-mosaic.fits -0.5s max gaussian-log -blue 1-mosaic.fits -0.5s max gaussian-log -png mosaic-color.png\\n```\\n\\nHere, `-red`, `-green`, and `-blue` assign FITS mosaics to display channels. Each receives its own `-0.5s max` range and `gaussian-log` stretch. `-png` names the final output. These choices control visualization, not the calibrated FITS pixel values.\\n\\nThe [official `mViewer` examples](https://github.com/Caltech-IPAC/Montage/blob/main/man/docs/mViewer.html) describe these interfaces. Berriman and Good\'s [visualization paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.02593) explains the scientific visualization context. The three single-band previews and the color preview are sibling consumers of the FITS mosaics: the color task does not need to wait for the preview PNGs.\\n\\n### Scientific parameters and execution settings are different\\n\\nThe command arguments above determine image processing and file products. The YAML also supplies Ak\xf4Flow settings such as `cpuLimit: 1000m` (one CPU), `memoryLimit: 1Gi`, the selected runtime, and `dependsOn`. Those govern execution placement, limits, and readiness; they are not flags passed to Montage. `workspaceSeedPath` identifies the packaged seed, while historical `simulation.durationSeconds` values support planning and do not measure a new real execution.\\n\\n## Reproduce the workload locally\\n\\nIf this is your first Ak\xf4Flow experiment, begin with [the Desktop walkthrough](/blog/from-a-dag-to-your-first-run). It teaches definitions, environments, scopes, plans, and runs with a tiny local workload.\\n\\nThe full Montage bundle adds a command-line preparation and execution path. Have **Git, Go 1.23 or newer, and Docker with linux/amd64 support** available. Keep at least **15 GiB** of free disk for the image and intermediate workspaces. Retention and concurrency affect the peak requirement. Apple Silicon runs this amd64 workload through emulation and may take substantially longer.\\n\\n### 1. Get the versioned example\\n\\n```bash\\ngit clone https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow-examples.git\\ncd akoflow-examples\\ngit checkout --detach d890c3171e3661890305b763b8fc3ede48bb61b4\\ncd scientific-workflows/montage-050\\n```\\n\\nPinning the checkout makes this walkthrough refer to a specific example revision. The [bundle README](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow-examples/blob/d890c3171e3661890305b763b8fc3ede48bb61b4/scientific-workflows/montage-050/README.md) documents its requirements and commands.\\n\\nThe bundle contains `workflow.yaml`, `environment.yaml`, `scope.yaml`, and `topology.yaml`, plus preparation, execution, and validation scripts. The workflow retains historical simulation durations as model inputs. Use the provided runner or daemon helper for the real workload; importing that source YAML alone is not the complete real-execution setup.\\n\\n### 2. Prepare the image and inputs\\n\\n```bash\\n./prepare.sh\\n```\\n\\nPreparation obtains the pinned image and Go dependencies, and checks the command/input contract. The image includes approximately 87 MiB of source FITS data, region headers, lookup tables, and the native Montage tools. There is no need to locate twelve observations manually.\\n\\nThe upstream image is pinned by digest:\\n\\n```text\\novvesley/akoflow-wf-montage@sha256:26b060b83b990557ea86ab40503cc8f17ab6016b5973e7e6f9201100a96440c9\\n```\\n\\nPreparation success establishes that the inputs and commands are available. It does not mean all scientific activities have executed.\\n\\n### 3. Execute the complete local DAG\\n\\n```bash\\n./run.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe standalone runner uses **two CPU slots** by default. Each activity becomes eligible once its dependencies finish and a slot is available. It copies the bundled input seed, executes the native commands with network access disabled, and writes activity observations under `outputs/`.\\n\\nThis path provisions no cloud machines and needs no remote HPC account. It is a substantial scientific workload: allow it to finish rather than interpreting a slow projection as a completed run.\\n\\n### 4. Validate and open the products\\n\\n```bash\\n./validate.sh\\n```\\n\\nInspect the final files in `outputs/`:\\n\\n| Product                                           | What it gives you                       |\\n| ------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\\n| `1-mosaic.fits`, `2-mosaic.fits`, `3-mosaic.fits` | Three 1800 \xd7 1800 scientific mosaics    |\\n| `1-mosaic.png`, `2-mosaic.png`, `3-mosaic.png`    | Monochrome visual previews              |\\n| `mosaic-color.png`                                | Combined color preview                  |\\n| `manifest.json`                                   | Generated artifacts and SHA-256 digests |\\n| `dag-validation.json`                             | Dependency-wave validation              |\\n| `stage-<activity>.json`                           | Per-activity observations               |\\n\\nThe validator checks all 58 activity records, FITS dimensions, and PNG headers. A successful manifest is published only after the workflow and final-file checks pass. Merely finding one intermediate image is insufficient to declare the experiment complete.\\n\\n### Inspect a daemon-managed run in Desktop\\n\\nThe bundle also supports a daemon helper that registers the local environment, builds the declared image, and submits the 58 activities to the engine. This advanced path requires a daemon whose API address and token you manage; follow [API connection setup](/docs/tutorials/api-access). The graphical Desktop installation keeps its internal credentials managed by its proxy\u2014do not assume that it exposes a shell token.\\n\\nFrom the example directory, with that daemon connection configured:\\n\\n```bash\\ngo run ../../tools/daemon-local -root ../.. -example scientific-workflows/montage-050 -wait=false\\n```\\n\\nThe command prints the submitted run ID and returns while execution continues. Connect Desktop to that same instance and open the run. Inspect its activity DAG, file handoffs, logs, and completion status. The daemon owns execution and transfers; the standalone runner\'s `outputs/` directory belongs to the separate standalone path.\\n\\n## What the completed hybrid experiment observed\\n\\nThe following results come from the preserved completion record for **`montage-docker-image-real-20261010-v2`**, dated by that run\'s identity. Its topology comprised ten GCP `e2-micro` instances, two `e2-medium` instances, and eight HPC activity assignments. This is a historical experiment, not the local two-slot configuration above.\\n\\n| Evidence                                              | Recorded result |\\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------: |\\n| Completed activities                                  |         58 / 58 |\\n| Failed activities                                     |               0 |\\n| Successful runtime handles                            |              58 |\\n| Missing dependency routes                             |               0 |\\n| Transfer-barrier violations                           |               0 |\\n| Unreleased consumer leases                            |               0 |\\n| Overlapping activities on single-lane micro instances |               0 |\\n| Final PNG dimensions                                  |     1800 \xd7 1800 |\\n\\nThe preserved record also distinguishes three timing scopes:\\n\\n| Timing                      |  Seconds | Approximately |\\n| --------------------------- | -------: | ------------: |\\n| Planned makespan            | 3,617.94 |      60.3 min |\\n| SimGrid makespan            | 3,617.50 |      60.3 min |\\n| Real API-reported makespan  | 6,924.25 |     115.4 min |\\n| Run timestamp wall interval | 6,949.00 |     115.8 min |\\n\\nThe real API-reported makespan was about **1.91\xd7 the planned estimate**. That is an observation from this run, not a general slowdown factor. Close agreement between the plan and simulation did not establish that the real distributed execution would match either prediction.\\n\\nThe accompanying results record identifies whole-workspace traffic and different planned edge volumes as a comparability limit. Summed transfer or compute work can overlap across activities and is not the same as wall-clock makespan. Likewise, a zero reported interference value cannot establish that physical contention was absent.\\n\\nThe recorded cloud compute-and-boot-disk lifecycle estimate was **USD 0.3532** at the experiment\'s list rates. It excludes network egress, public IPv4, reusable image storage, discounts, and tax, and assigns no calibrated monetary tariff to HPC. It is an estimate, not a provider invoice or a current price quote. Other cost fields in the JSON use different task/window scopes and should not be treated as equivalent bills.\\n\\n[Download the result summary and its limitations](/downloads/blog/montage-050/observed-results.json). The repository preserves the original [completion record](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow/blob/main/experiments/montage-micro-hpc-e2/docker-image-real-v2-completion.json) and [detailed results](https://github.com/UFFeScience/akoflow/blob/main/experiments/montage-micro-hpc-e2/docker-image-real-v2-results.json).\\n\\n## What is\u2014and is not\u2014verified for the local bundle\\n\\nThe checked-in local image contract was validated on **2026-10-20**: twelve FITS inputs, 58 activities, and 138 named handoffs. That record explicitly states `fullExecutionPerformed: false`. Its separate daemon snapshot recorded a running workflow with zero completed activities at capture time. These are historical snapshots; neither establishes the current state of another instance.\\n\\nThe completed hybrid run supplies the mosaic and the results in this article. To claim a completed reproduction on your machine, retain your own successful validator output, activity records, and output manifest. The [local showcase page](/docs/showcase/scientific-workflows/montage-050) links the checked-in contract and daemon evidence.\\n\\n## Keep the experiment with the picture\\n\\nThe mosaic is the visible result, but the research artifact includes more: input identity, native commands, the dependency graph, resource assignments, file transfers, and output digests. Together, they explain how the image was produced and where a later execution differs.\\n\\nAk\xf4Flow\'s original paper provides the research context: Ferreira et al., **Ak\xf4Flow: um Middleware para execu\xe7\xe3o de Workflows cient\xedficos em m\xfaltiplos ambientes conteinerizados**, SBBD 2024, pp. 27\u201339. [Read the publication](https://doi.org/10.5753/sbbd.2024.241126).\\n\\nContinue with the [complete local example](/docs/showcase/scientific-workflows/montage-050), the [Desktop first experiment](/blog/from-a-dag-to-your-first-run), or [planning alternatives](/docs/guides/workflows/planning). When comparing placements, preserve the scientific workload and change the execution environment deliberately, so the measurements remain interpretable."}]}}')}}]);